== The Text Opened and the Point Proved

#align(center)[PRECIOUS REMEDIES #emph[AGAINST] SATAN’S DEVICES.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[2 Corinthians 2:11]

    #text(style: "italic")[So that Satan should not get an advantage over us; for we are not ignorant of his devices.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

In the fifth #emph[verse] the apostle shows that the incestuous man had by his incest saddened those precious souls whom God would not have saddened. Souls that walk sinfully are Hazaels to the godly, and draw many sighs and tears from them. Jeremiah weeps in secret for Judah’s sins, and Paul cannot speak of those #emph[belly-gods] with dry eyes (Phil 3:18). And Lot’s righteous soul was burdened,#footnote[Καταπονούμενον ἐβασάνιζεν.] vexed, and racked by the filthy Sodomites (2 Pet 2:7, 8). Every sinful Sodomite was a Hazael to his eyes, a Hadadrimmon to his heart. Gracious souls are accustomed to mourn for other men’s sins as well as their own, and for the souls#footnote[Ps 119:136, 158.] and sins of those who make a mock of sin, and a jest of damning their own souls. Guilt or grief is all that gracious souls get by fellowship with worldly souls.

In the sixth #emph[verse] he shows that the punishment inflicted on the incestuous man was sufficient, and therefore they should not refuse to receive him, since he had repented and sorrowed for his former faults and follies. It is not for the honor of Christ, the credit of the gospel, nor the good of souls, for professing Christians to be like those#footnote[Foxe, #emph[Acts and Monuments], fol. 1392.] bloody wretches who burned at the stake some who had recanted, saying that they would send them out of the world while they were in a good mind.

In #emph[verses] 7, 8, 9, and 10 the apostle stirs up the church to forgive him, to comfort him, and to confirm their love toward him, so that he might not be swallowed up with excessive sorrow, for Satan was going about to mix the detestable darnel weed of despair with the godly sorrow of a pure, penitent heart. It was a sweet saying of one: Let a man#footnote[#emph[Doleat & de dolore gaudeat,] Jerome.] grieve for his sin, and then rejoice in his grief. That sorrow for sin which keeps the soul from looking toward the mercy-seat, and which keeps Christ and the soul apart, or which makes the soul unfit for the communion of saints, is a sinful sorrow.

In the eleventh #emph[verse] he gives another reason to move them to show pity and mercy to the penitent sinner, who was mourning and groaning under his sin and misery, that is, “so that Satan should not get an advantage over us; for we are not ignorant of his devices.” Now a little to open the words.

#emph[So that Satan should not get an advantage over us,] #footnote[Πλεονεκτηθῶμεν.] that is, so that Satan should not over-reach us. The Greek word signifies to have more than belongs to one. The comparison is taken from the greedy merchant, who seeks and takes every opportunity to cheat and deceive others. Satan is that wily merchant who devours not widows’ houses, but most men’s souls.

#emph[For we are not ignorant of] Satan’s #emph[devices,] or plots, or machinations, or stratagems.#footnote[Νοήματα.] He is only a Christian in name who has no personal experience of Satan’s stratagems, his set and composed machinations, his artfully molded methods, his plots, darts, and depths, by which he outwitted our first parents, and by which he always fits each of us with a cheating bargain, as he sees fit.

The main observation that I will draw from these words is this:

#emph[That Satan has his various devices to deceive, entangle, and ruin the souls of men.]

I will first prove the point; secondly, show you his various devices; thirdly, the remedies against his devices; and fourthly, how it comes about that he has so many different devices to deceive, entangle, and ruin the souls of men. Fifthly, I will lay down some propositions concerning Satan and his devices. For the proof of the point, take these few Scriptures. Eph 6:11: “Put on the full armor of God, so that you#super[+] can stand against the wiles of the devil.” The Greek word#footnote[Μεθοδείας.] that is here rendered #emph[wiles] is a notably emphatic word.

1\. It signifies such snares as are laid behind one, such treacheries as come upon one’s back unawares. It denotes the methods, or ambushes, of that old subtle serpent, who, like Dan’s adder in the path, bites the heels of travelers, and so passes his venom to the head and heart. The word signifies an ambush, or a stratagem of war, by which the enemy sets upon a man #emph[ex insidiis,] unawares.

2\. It signifies such snares as are set to#footnote[a μετά & ὁδός.] catch one on one’s road. A man walks along his road and does not think of it; suddenly he is caught by thieves, or falls into a pit, etc.

3\. It signifies such snares as are purposely, artfully, and craftily set for taking the prey at the greatest possible advantage. The Greek μεθοδείας, being derived from μετά and ὁδός, properly signifies a waylaying, a circumvention, or a going about, as those do who seek after their prey. Julian by his craft drew more people from the faith than all his persecuting predecessors could by their cruelty. So Satan does more hurt in his sheepskin than by roaring like a lion.

Take one Scripture more for the proof of the point, and that is in the second letter to Timothy, the second chapter, and the last verse: “and that they may recover themselves out of the snare of the devil, who has taken them captive at his will.” The Greek word that is here rendered #emph[recover themselves] signifies to awake#footnote[Ἀνανήψωσιν.] themselves. The apostle alludes to one who is asleep or drunk, who is to be awakened and restored to his senses. And the Greek word that is here rendered #emph[taken captive] signifies to be taken alive.#footnote[ἐζωγρημένοι.] The word is properly a word of war, and signifies to be taken alive, as soldiers are taken alive in war, or as birds are taken alive and ensnared in the fowler’s net. Satan has snares for the wise and snares for the simple, snares for hypocrites and snares for the upright, snares for bold souls and snares for timid souls, snares for the rich and snares for the poor, snares for the aged and snares for the young, etc. Happy are those souls that are not taken and held in the snares that he has laid. Take one proof more, and then I will proceed to the opening of the point, and that is in Rev 2:24: “But I say to the rest of you#super[+] in Thyatira, as many as do not hold#footnote[Pareus on this passage. 1 Tim 4:1.] to this teaching and have not known the depths of Satan, as they call them: I will place no further burden upon you; only hold fast until I come.” Those poor souls called their opinions the depths of God, when in fact they were the depths of Satan. You#super[+] call your opinions depths, and so they are; but they are such depths as Satan has brought out of hell. They are the whisperings and hissings of that serpent, not the inspirations of God.
